  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThe general rule of thumb this year is that it has been a very warm \ncalendar year, and in some locations back to last autumn. And, \nthis will be the rule over the next several days as well. If you\nwant to see some statistics on this, we will have a post on our\nsocial media pages on how warm it has been a little later this \nmorning. In the meantime, back to our regularly scheduled \nforecast discussion.\n\nA weak shortwave is now working its way through Idaho, and will \ncross Wyoming today. The wave is rather moisture starved, with most \nof any moisture remaining north of the area across Montana. There \ncould be just enough lift for a stray shower across northern Wyoming \nthrough around 2 pm, but the chance everywhere except the mountains \nis less than 1 out of 5. Most areas will just see some passing \nclouds. The main impact most areas will see is a gusty wind. The 700 \nmillibar winds are not sufficient for high wind (only topping out at \naround 30 to 35 knots). However, there will be a gusty wind for many \nlocations, Much of the area has at least a 1 out 2 chance of wind \ngusts over 30 mph. The greater than 1 out of 2 chance of wind gusts \nover 50 mph is largely restricted to the higher elevations though. \nThe peak wind would be early in the afternoon, during the time of \nmaximum mixing and an assist from an 100 knot jet streak passing \nacross northern Wyoming at this time. Wind should decrease after \nsunset as the shortwave moves away and the atmosphere decouples. \nAnd, it will be another day of mild temperatures m with many \nlocations East of the Divide seeing high temperatures in the 60s.\n\nWeather concerns will be few to none Wednesday and Thursday as a \nridge axis moves over Wyoming, bringing another period of above\nnormal temperatures and light to moderate wind. Friday looks \ndry as well, although the next approaching system from the west \nwill tighten the pressure gradient and bring breezier conditions\nto the favored southwest flow locations like Casper. With \nrelative humidity falling to around 20 percent, we could see \nelevated fire weather in the afternoon in some locations.\n\nThe aforementioned weather system mentioned in the last paragraph, a \nPacific trough and front, should approach the area on Saturday. The \ncurrent forecast shows some showers moving in later Friday night. \nThis looks a bit too fast though, most showers should hold off \nuntil Saturday and especially Saturday afternoon and evening. \nThis system will likely be weakening as it approaches with the \nmain forcing remaining south of Wyoming. As a result, \nprecipitation amounts should remain light. The only areas with a\ngreater than 1 in 2 chance of over a tenth of an inch of QPF \nare the western mountains and the chance of over a half of an \ninch is basically zero at this point. With the westerly movement\nof the system, downsloping flow should dominate East of the \nDivide with only isolated to widely scattered showers (less than\n1 in 4 chance). \n\nOther than a few lingering showers in western Wyoming, mainly dry \nconditions should return on Sunday and last into Monday. \nTemperatures will cool somewhat behind the front. However, with\nthe Pacific origin of the air, temperatures should remain near \nto somewhat above normal. Guidance is showing something \npotentially more interesting for the middle of next week. This \nis still a long way out though, and as we all know, a lot can \nchange over the course of a week.",
